Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 12:44:01 +0800
Subject: [14/14] potentials: literature-based mixed LJ cross terms, and why
 they also fail

Replaces the hand-picked LJ cross terms (eps 0.20-0.30 eV, sigma 2.00 A) with
Lorentz-Berthelot mixing of published single-element parameters
(Si 3.83 A/0.01743 eV, Ti 2.93 A/0.0250 eV, Au 2.57 A/0.0390 eV), keeping the
real Si Tersoff and Au EAM. Same flat structure, 300 K NVT for 50 ps:

  old: PotEng drops 2206 eV, dPE/dt -6.4 eV/ps, 743 Ti atoms inside |z|<20 A
  new: PotEng drops  311 eV, dPE/dt -4.1 eV/ps, 373 Ti atoms inside |z|<20 A
       BUT 4320 Si atoms (26 %) end up beyond |z|>105 A, i.e. the slabs expand
       into the vacuum -- the layers are no longer bound.

So the two failure modes are 'violently reactive' and 'not bound'. There is no
physically sensible value for a Lennard-Jones cross term between Ti and Si,
because the real bond is chemical (silicide, ~1 eV/atom). This closes the
'fit the LJ better' route and shows that a trustworthy TBR for this stack needs
a real multi-element potential (NEP/MEAM); the state of the art for Si-Ti is the
NEP of arXiv:2511.16646, which needs the GPUMD plugin and cannot run on stock
LAMMPS 29Sep2021.

Also adds docs/HANDOFF_STATE.md: a context-loss-proof state file (what is
established, what is in flight, next steps, open human decisions).
